 and throughout the  Middle East the Israeli Jews are viewed  a very common way of describing them is  as a Western colonizing force they're  actually often described as the last  wave of Western colonization and then  backed by the United States you talk  about Hollywood and television why  well that's these are more products of  globalization and but image are we  transmitting around the world well I  think that people have a distorted view  of the United States because what do  they see what is the United States for  the rest of the world they see our  president they see our supermodels they  see our multinationals and and that's  the slice of America that they see so  it's you know on the one hand Hollywood  creates this image of glamour this idea  that everybody here lives these lives of  indolence and luxury when in fact you  know many Americans are not like that  and worked very hard and so I do think  there's a that's part of the feelings of  envy and desire that global markets  perpetuate you say that Americans expect  the rest of the world to adopt democracy  overnight when we as a country didn't  yes this is one of the misunderstandings  that I frequently get about my book  people ask me if I'm you know against  school and  globalization and the answer is  definitely not I'm very much in favor of  globalization I don't know I think it'd  be futile to be against it  and they also ask whether I'm anti  markets or anti democracy and I am very  much in favor of promoting markets and  democracy globally both of them but  there are many different versions of  free-market democracy and I think that  we are exporting the wrong version in  fact I think we're where basically we've  been for the last 20 years promoting a  caricature a free-market democracy so -  on the market side there was no Western  nation today that has anything close to  a less a fair system I mean we have  progressive taxation and antitrust laws  and Social Security but for the last 20  years we've you know been urging poor  countries to adopt a kind of  bare-knuckled version of capitalism that  Europe and the u.s. abandoned long ago  and it's the same with democracy you  know for the last 20 years  I'm the American US government has been  urging the poor countries of the world  to hold elections to have you know  implement immediate universal suffrage  but yes in our own history was one in  which we disenfranchised the poor for  for four generations and more  importantly I mean I think that's out of  the question  you know property your exclusions from  the suffrage I'm not in favor of that  but I think more importantly democracy  in the West means much more than just  unrestrained majority rule which i think  is what we've been promoting I think  democracy is also about  constitutionalism and minority  protections and property protections  human rights I think a lot more thought  is needed than just shipping out ballot  boxes for elections which you don't  brought people like Milosevic  to power this is the cover of the book  it's called world on fire and our guest  has been Amy Chua Yale University law  professor thank you very much for  joining thank you for having me
 

